WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.073
Let's walk through this proof: Number of odd-degree vertices is even.

00:00:05.073 --> 00:00:10.388
The sum of all degrees is always twice the number of edges.

00:00:10.388 --> 00:00:13.489
Handshaking Lemma: every edge has two endpoints,

00:00:13.489 --> 00:00:16.590
contributing 2 to the total degree sum.

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:20.455
Therefore, the total sum must be even.

00:00:20.455 --> 00:00:23.720
Any integer multiplied by 2 is even.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.701
Split the sum into even-degree and odd-degree vertices.

00:00:27.701 --> 00:00:31.082
Partitioning the set of vertices into those with

00:00:31.082 --> 00:00:34.040
even degree and those with odd degree.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.940
The sum of even degrees is itself even.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:41.240
A sum of even numbers is always even.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:45.964
So the sum of odd degrees must also be even.

00:00:45.964 --> 00:00:50.500
If (Even + X) is even, then X must be even.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.492
To get an even sum, you must

00:00:53.492 --> 00:00:55.885
have an even number of odd terms.

00:00:55.885 --> 00:00:58.960
An odd number added to itself an odd number

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.036
of times is odd; an odd number added to

00:01:02.036 --> 00:01:04.770
itself an even number of times is even.

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:07.504
Thus, to get an even sum from odd

00:01:07.504 --> 00:01:10.239
numbers, you need an even number of them.

00:01:10.239 --> 00:01:12.631
Notice that a step is skipped here.

00:01:12.631 --> 00:01:16.048
Explain that if you sum k odd numbers, the sum

00:01:16.048 --> 00:01:19.124
is even if and only if k is even.

00:01:19.124 --> 00:01:22.200
So, in summary: The logic is sound, but S6

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.276
skips the final parity argument connecting the sum of

00:01:25.276 --> 00:01:28.010
odd numbers to the count of those numbers.
